<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-elementary-matrices" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Elementary Matrices</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Row reduction has so far been a procedure we carry out <em>on</em> a matrix. In this
      section we see that each elementary row operation can itself be encoded as a matrix,
      so that performing the operation becomes multiplication by that matrix. This turns
      the matrix inversion algorithm into a factorization.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-elementary-matrix-definition">
    <title>Elementary Matrices and Row Operations</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-elementary-matrix">
      <title>Elementary Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          An <term>elementary matrix</term> is a matrix obtained from an identity matrix
          by performing <em>a single</em> elementary row operation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-eros">
      <title>Reminder: Elementary Row Operations</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Interchange two rows.</p></li>
          <li><p>Multiply a row by a nonzero number.</p></li>
          <li><p>Add a (nonzero) multiple of one row to a different row.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="eg-elementary-matrices">
      <title>Three Elementary Matrices</title>
      <p>
        Each of the following is obtained from <m>I_4</m> by a single elementary row
        operation:
        <md>
          E = \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0
          \end{bmatrix},
          \quad
          F = \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
          \end{bmatrix},
          \quad
          G = \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            -3 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Specifically,
        <md>
          <mrow>
            I_4 \amp \xrightarrow{\;R_2 \leftrightarrow R_4\;} E,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            I_4 \amp \xrightarrow{\;-2R_3\;} F,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            I_4 \amp \xrightarrow{\;-3R_1 + R_3\;} G.
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-left-multiplication">
      <title>The Effect of Left Multiplication</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let us examine the effect of left multiplication of
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 3 \\ 4 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          by the elementary matrices <m>E</m>, <m>F</m>, and <m>G</m> of
          <xref ref="eg-elementary-matrices" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            EA = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 3 \\ 4 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 4 \amp 4 \\ 3 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          which is <m>A</m> with rows <m>2</m> and <m>4</m> interchanged.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            FA = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 3 \\ 4 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \\ -6 \amp -6 \\ 4 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          which is <m>A</m> with row <m>3</m> multiplied by <m>-2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            GA = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              -3 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 3 \\ 4 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \\ 4 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          which is <m>A</m> with <m>-3</m> times row <m>1</m> added to row <m>3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <observation xml:id="obs-left-multiplication">
      <p>
        Left multiplication of a matrix by an elementary matrix induces the same row
        operation that was used to obtain that elementary matrix from the identity matrix.
      </p>
    </observation>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-inverses-of-elementary-matrices">
    <title>Inverses of Elementary Matrices</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-inverse-of-elementary">
      <title>Inverting an Elementary Matrix by Inspection</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Without using the matrix inversion algorithm, find the inverse of the elementary
          matrix
          <md>
            G = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              -3 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Hint.</term> What row operation can be applied to <m>G</m> to transform it
          to <m>I_4</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>G</m> was obtained from <m>I_4</m> by the row operation
          <m>-3R_1 + R_3</m>. The row operation that undoes it is <m>+3R_1 + R_3</m>, so
          applying that inverse row operation to <m>I_4</m> gives <m>G^{-1}</m>:
          <md>
            I_4 \xrightarrow[\text{row operation}]{\;-3R_1 + R_3\;} G
            \xrightarrow[\text{inverse row operation}]{\;+3R_1 + R_3\;}
            G^{-1} = \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
              3 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
              0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <observation xml:id="obs-inverse-elementary">
      <p>
        For finding the inverse of an elementary matrix <m>E</m>, we can apply to <m>I</m>
        the inverse of the row operation applied to <m>I</m> to obtain <m>E</m>. This will
        be more efficient than the matrix inversion algorithm.
      </p>
      <p>
        In particular, every elementary matrix is invertible, and its inverse is again an
        elementary matrix.
      </p>
    </observation>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-product-of-elementary-matrices">
    <title>A Matrix as a Product of Elementary Matrices</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-product-of-elementary">
      <title>Factoring a Matrix into Elementary Matrices</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Express
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -4 \\ -3 \amp -6 \amp 13 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          as a product of elementary matrices and, if possible, find <m>A^{-1}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We need to keep track of the elementary row operations performed in the matrix
          inversion algorithm. Each step below records the elementary matrix
          <m>E_i</m> obtained by applying that step's row operation to <m>I_3</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                -3 \amp -6 \amp 13 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right]
              \amp \xrightarrow{R_2 + 3R_1}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{R_3 \leftrightarrow R_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp -1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{-R_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
                1 \amp 2 \amp -4 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{R_1 - 2R_2}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \xrightarrow{R_2 + 2R_3}
              \left[ \begin{array}{ccc|ccc}
                1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
                0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 6 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\
                0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 0
              \end{array} \right]
              = \left[ \, I_3 \mid A^{-1} \, \right].
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The corresponding elementary matrices are
          <md>
            E_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            E_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            E_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          <md>
            E_4 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad
            E_5 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          By <xref ref="obs-left-multiplication" text="type-global" />, applying these five
          row operations in order is the same as multiplying on the left by
          <m>E_1</m>, then <m>E_2</m>, and so on. Since the reduction carried <m>A</m> to
          <m>I</m>,
          <md>
            \left( E_5 E_4 E_3 E_2 E_1 \right) A = I.
          </md>
          Multiplying both sides on the right by <m>A^{-1}</m> gives
          <md>
            \left( E_5 E_4 E_3 E_2 E_1 \right) \underbrace{A A^{-1}}_{I} = I A^{-1},
          </md>
          so
          <md>
            A^{-1} = E_5 E_4 E_3 E_2 E_1
            = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\ 6 \amp 2 \amp -1 \\ 3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          agreeing with the right half of the reduced augmented matrix above.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, taking inverses of both sides and using
          <xref ref="thm-inverses-transposes-products" text="type-global" /> (3),
          <md>
            A = \left( E_5 E_4 E_3 E_2 E_1 \right)^{-1}
              = E_1^{-1} E_2^{-1} E_3^{-1} E_4^{-1} E_5^{-1},
          </md>
          that is,
          <md>
            A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ -3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}
                \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
                \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          where each factor was obtained from the corresponding <m>E_i</m> by
          <xref ref="obs-inverse-elementary" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
